{
  "status": "ok",
  "data": {
    "query": "Liu He Washington trade negotiations February March 2019 enforcement mechanism",
    "search_window": {
      "from": "2018-11-13",
      "to": "2019-03-13"
    },
    "sources": {
      "grounding": {
        "generic": [
          {
            "url": "https://www.politico.com/story/2019/02/13/china-trump-xi-trade-talks-1165792",
            "title": "China aims to run out the clock to get Trump and Xi to the table ...",
            "snippets": [
              "# China aims to run out the clock to get Trump and Xi to the table on trade\nBut Trump’s actions and words, however, are sending signals to Chinese officials that they might be able to get away with reverting to their playbook of making minor concessions",
              "The president told reporters on Tuesday that he wants a “real deal” with China, not one that “cosmetically looks good for a year.”\n\n“China wants to make a deal very badly,” he said.\n\nHowever, during the last round of talks in Washington, China withheld any major concessions that would have addressed structural issues like forced technology transfer and subsidies, according to sources close to the talks",
              "One source who declined to be named because of the sensitive nature of the negotiations said China has only made offers in line with Xi’s speech at last April’s Boao Forum.\n\nAt the time, he made commitments to ease restrictions on foreign banks and insurance companies, lower barriers on auto imports, and increase enforcement of intellectual property.",
              "Beijing has also ramped up efforts to take simple and easy actions, such as offering to buy more American commodities like soybeans and natural gas. Chinese Vice Premier Liu He, who is leading talks for China, surprised the U.S. side by telling Trump directly in a Jan. 31 Oval Office meeting that Beijing would buy an additional 5 million metric tons of U.S. soybeans",
              "Heavier demands, such as addressing tech transfer and subsidy policies, could take years to carry out, even if Beijing was in a mindset to do so. In the meantime, negotiators have less than 20 days to meet a March 1 deadline to reach some kind of a deal, or at least a ceasefire, before the U.S. escalates tariffs on $200 billion worth of goods. That number represents around 40 percent of items the U.S. imports from China",
              "A key part of the deal will be holding China to any commitments it makes. Lighthizer is working to develop an enforcement mechanism that could include re-imposition of tariffs and the revival of a now-lapsed safeguard measure to stop surges of Chinese imports that Beijing agreed to when it joined the World Trade Organization, according to two sources close to the discussions. He’s also expressed reluctance at rolling back tariffs beyond refraining from any additional escalation, those source said",
              "On Tuesday, Trump appeared to soften his administration’s previously hardened stance, saying the deadline could be extended if the two sides appear close to a deal.",
              "“They’re going to spend, we hope, considerable amounts on time on areas where they’ve made much less progress, particularly those around forced technology transfer, industrial policy issues, and the enforcement mechanisms of a potential agreement,” said Jeremie Waterman, vice president for greater China at the U.S. Chamber of Commerce",
              "On one front, the president has stuck to punishing China for past actions. Trump is expected to sign an executive order as soon as this week banning the use of Chinese telecommunication equipment made by Huawei and ZTE on U.S. wireless networks for national security reasons.\n\nThere’s also the question of what China will get in exchange for making any concessions, whether it’s more commodity purchases or the structural changes the U.S. is demanding"
            ]
          },
          {
            "url": "https://www.scmp.com/news/china/diplomacy/article/2184590/china-says-washington-trade-talks-were-frank-and-made-important",
            "title": "China and US upbeat on progress after Washington trade talks",
            "snippets": [
              "# China and US upbeat on progress after Washington trade talks\nBoth sides signalled developments on bilateral sticking points beyond their trade imbalance – with a focus on structural issues and an enforcement mechanism – after talks led by Chinese Vice-Premier Liu He and US Trade Representative Robert Lighthizer concluded on Thursday. Follow-up discussions are slated for early February in Beijing",
              "{\"alternativeHeadline\":\"China and US upbeat on progress after Washington trade talks\",\"articleBody\":\"“Both sides agree this agreement is worth nothing – if we can get an agreement, it’s worth nothing without enforcement,” he said. One possibility would be a memorandum of understanding, Bloomberg reported. China’s foreign ministry spokesman Geng Shuang told reporters on Friday that both sides had a clear timeline and road map for the next steps, adding that Xi was “also willing to maintain close contact with Trump through various methods”. In a statement published by state news agency Xinhua, the Chinese delegation said the “frank, concrete, and constructive discussions have made important progress”. Trump earlier hailed the “tremendous progress” he had seen from China towards reforming its laws on forced technology transfers and intellectual property protection, as well as an agreement for China to buy 5 million tonnes of US soybeans. Donald Trump hails trade talks’ progress, says meeting Xi Jinping ‘possible’ on trip to meet Kim Jong-un But a key question was still a mechanism for enforcement – a word Lighthizer repeated multiple times on Thursday – to ensure China followed through on structural commitments, observers said. Alan Beebe, president of the American Chamber of Commerce in China, said the promise to buy more US soybeans was “really low-hanging fruit”, and that a binding agreement with an enforcement mechanism for Chinese structural reform was needed. That could mean the use of tariffs to ensure progress or seeing “sufficient teeth” in regulations and laws for structural reforms, he said. “What we would consider a best-case scenario is that there’s something written in binding form with sufficient specificity, with clear timelines on the sort of comprehensive steps that China would take in order to resolve many of the long-standing issues – but we recognise that it’s not easy,” he said. But analysts were optimistic, noting the latest talks reflected considerable progress towards a deal, even if implementation would take time. “There is a high possibility of reaching a deal before the deadline,” said Li Mingjiang, an associate professor at the S. Rajaratnam School of International Studies at Nanyang Technological University in Singapore. “But implementation will be a big challenge if China and the US can reach a satisfactory agreement. It would be an all-encompassing deal ... this problem cannot be resolved over a short period of time.” Liu Weidong, a Sino-US expert at the Chinese Academy of Social Sciences, said both sides may not have fully revealed their positions in negotiations yet. “While they were still reaching out to find the bottom line this time around, they are more clear on it than they were before,” he said. “The work that needs to be done now is to further understand their respective situations, as the basis for their decision-making.” Donald Trump says trade war will ‘hopefully’ be resolved before March – but not before he’s met Xi Jinping Fu Mengzi, vice-president of the China Institutes of Contemporary International Relations in Beijing, added that progress had been made, but more negotiation was needed on the key issues of forced technology transfers and intellectual property protection. “If both sides can have a pragmatic and active attitude, the February negotiations in Beijing may still yield an agreement,” he said. “But some structural problems will need a transition plan, and it is not realistic to expect them to be resolved overnight.” For US businesses in China, Beebe said optimism remained that trade tensions would ease. “Most people are hopeful, but we advise our member companies ... that hope is not a strategy,” he said. “Of course it’s good to hope for the best, but in a way, one needs to plan for the worst.”\",\"articleSection\":\"Diplomacy\",\"author\":{\"name\":\"Sarah Zheng\",\"sameAs\":\"https://www.scmp.com/author/sarah-zheng\"},\"dateCreated\":\"2019-02-01T10:41:17+08:00\",\"dateModified\":\"2019-02-01T23:23:36+08:00\",\"datePublished\":\"2019-02-01T10:45:15+08:00\",\"genre\":\"News\",\"hasPart\":{\"isAccessibleForFree\":\"False\"},\"headline\":\"China and US upbeat on progress after Washington trade talks\",\"inLanguage\":\"en-GB\",\"isAccessibleForFree\":\"False\",\"locationCreated\":{\"name\":\"Beijing\"},\"publisher\":{\"name\":\"South China Morning Post\"}}"
            ]
          },
          {
            "url": "https://www.cnbc.com/2019/02/19/us-china-trade-war-deal-needs-enforcement-says-chamber-of-commerce.html",
            "title": "US Chamber of Commerce: China trade deal 'fails' if Washington ...",
            "snippets": [
              "Any trade deal between the U.S. and China will need an enforcement mechanism, an American business association official said on Tuesday.\nlogo\nBusiness\nTech\nVideo\nPRO",
              "# US Chamber of Commerce: China trade deal 'fails' if Washington doesn't win enforcement measures\n- Any trade deal between the U.S. and China will need enforcement mechanisms, an American business association official said.\n- This week, Chinese Vice Premier Liu He will visit Washington on Thursday and Friday to continue trade negotiations with the U.S., the Commerce Ministry in Beijing said",
              "Chinese Vice Premier and lead trade negotiator Liu He, right, reaches to shake hands with U.S. Trade Representative Robert Lighthizer before the opening session of trade negotiations at the Diaoyutai State Guesthouse in Beijing, Thursday, Feb.",
              "Pledges from Beijing to buy more American goods and fix some economic structural issues are meaningless victories for the U.S. if Donald Trump's administration doesn't also create ways to enforce a trade agreement with China, according to a top official from the U.S. Chamber of Commerce",
              "That comes as Chinese negotiators ready for the next round of trade talks, set to begin Tuesday in Washington. So far, reports indicate, the two countries have found common ground on China decreasing its trade surplus with the U.S. through more purchases, but sticking points remain on issues such as intellectual property theft and the subsidies Beijing gives to its domestic firms",
              "\"Without enforcement, this deal fails,\" he told CNBC's Eunice Yoon in Beijing. \"Implementation and enforcement are going to be two key elements — so you need to have implementation, you need to have follow-through, but you need to have enforcement mechanisms that will ensure that both sides have trust that this deal is sustaining and verifiable.\"",
